<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palermo Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1185">1185</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> Cathedral is the cathedral of the Sicilian capital, rebuilt from 1185 by Archbishop Walter Ophamil on the site of an earlier church that had served as a mosque under Muslim rule. Roger II was crowned in the earlier building on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Day 1130, and the present cathedral holds the royal tombs of Roger II, Henry VI, Constance and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>. It is part of a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palermo-cathedral.jpg" alt="Palermo Cathedral" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julian Lupyan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palermo_Cathedral_Facade.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The cathedral is dedicated to the Assumption of the Virgin Mary and is the church of the Roman Catholic Archdiocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>. Walter Ophamil, the Norman archbishop of Palermo and minister of King William II, had it erected in 1185 on the area of an earlier Byzantine basilica. By tradition that church was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> turned it into a mosque after conquering the city in the 9th century. A Qur'an verse is carved on a column of the southern portico, which belonged to the original basilica and the mosque. Ophamil is buried in the crypt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The building mixes styles because of centuries of additions. The upper orders of the corner towers date from the 14th and 15th centuries, and the Gothic-Catalan southern portico was erected around 1465. Charles III and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> were both crowned in the cathedral, the latter in December 1713.</p><p>The present neoclassical appearance comes from work between 1781 and 1801, supervised by Ferdinando Fuga and Giuseppe Venanzio Marvuglia. Marvuglia's changes were more radical than Fuga's plans, and the great retable by Gagini was destroyed, its sculptures moved elsewhere in the building. In 1801 the astronomer Giuseppe Piazzi, who discovered the asteroid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceres">Ceres</a>, built a meridian line on the floor, where an image of the sun, projected through a small hole in a dome, crosses a bronze line at solar noon. It was meant to standardise time and to fix the date of the spring equinox for Easter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the first two chapels of the right aisle stand the tombs of Roger II, his daughter Constance I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, her husband Emperor Henry VI and their son Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>, with the burials of Constance of Aragon and Peter II of Sicily. The four main sarcophagi are of porphyry and are described as the first medieval free-standing secular tombs in the West. Odo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a>, half-brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, was buried in the cathedral in 1097. The chapel of Saint Rosalia, patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, holds a 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> urn that is an object of devotion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palermo Cathedral” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the papal schism of 1130 he supported Antipope Anacletus II, who granted him a royal title, and Roger II was crowned in <b>Palermo Cathedral</b> on Christmas Day 1130.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the papal schism of 1130 he supported Antipope Anacletus II, who granted him a royal title in return, and on Christmas Day 1130 Roger was crowned in <b>Palermo Cathedral</b>, in the building that stood before the present one.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li></ul></section>
